public int maxUniqueSplit(String s) {
    int n = s.length();
    int[] dp = new int[n];
    Set<String> seen = new HashSet<>();
    seen.add(s);
    dp[0] = 1;
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) != s.charAt(j)) {
                String sub = s.substring(j, i + 1);
                if (!seen.contains(sub)) {
                    seen.add(sub);
                    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return dp[n - 1];
}
```
This is a dynamic programming solution with memoization. The memoization is implemented using a hash set `seen` that keeps track of all unique substrings encountered so far. The algorithm loops over the input string `s` and for each index `i`, it checks all substring indices `j` such that `s[i] != s[j]`. If `s[i] != s[j]` and `s[i:]` is not in `seen`, it means that `s[i:j+1]` is a valid split and we update the maximum number of unique substrings found so far by adding 1 to the previous maximum. Finally, the maximum number of unique substrings found is returned.

Note that the time complexity of this solution is O(n^2) where n is the length of the input string `s`. This is because the longest substring that can be split is the entire input string, and we have a nested loop that checks all possible substring indices.

